so to me the idea of spacing workouts  throughout the day rather than doing  them all in one go makes a lot of  intuitive sense this is more similar to  how you would have trained in the wild  it's what our bodies evolved to do we  wouldn't have just been inert for 23  hours and then had one hour of intensive  activity that just wouldn't make any  sense instead we'd be constantly  tracking animals climbing trees running  through woods etc so we'd be always kind  of active instead of just briefly  extremely active at the same time I find  that the idea of doing shorter workouts  and multiple times throughout the day  fits into my schedule a lot more easily  I often don't have an hour to not only  work out but then the half hour I need  to get there and get back to have a  shower from sweaty etc 20 minutes over  lunch and then of 10 minutes in the  evening makes a lot more sense and the  great thing is about that is if I miss a  workout it's not the end of the world  because I've still got two other  workouts but I couldn't really find any  useful information online as to whether  or not this was a good idea I found  several studies all saying things like  you can still improve your general  fitness surprise-surprise by training  sporadically throughout the day in this  way they're talking more things like  very basic fitness rehabilitation almost  but that's not really what I'm  interested in I'm interested in  hypertrophy in increasing strength  mobility flexibility skills training etc  can you get those kinds of gains from 10  and 20 minute workouts throughout the  day so there are no concrete answers on  the web I decided I'd use my own fitness  knowledge as well as just testing it  myself to see if it works so for the  last month in a bit  I've been training about three or four  times a day for 10 to 20 minutes maybe  even less sometimes and I did a bit of a  deep dive I did lots of research into  different topics surrounding this  subject to see if I could come up some  logical conclusions and I'm gonna  present those to you now so first things  first in terms of my own personal  experience it's been fantastic training  in those shorter periods has meant I've  been far more consistent with my  workouts and at the same time I've seen  actually growth and strength  improvements that I didn't expect to see  and I probably wouldn't have seen if I'd  stuck to my usual training regime of  course any kind of change might trigger  growth because the body adapts to  whatever you're giving it but in many  ways it kind of makes sense if you've  been watching Jeff Nichols videos he's  been talking about training full-body  five times a week  saying how this improves protein  synthesis because it increases presence  is more times throughout the week for  each muscle group talking about how it  improves blood flow to those muscle  areas and talking about how it allows  you to do more repetitions with good  quality because you're having a long  rest in between so you can increase the  volume without seeing a drop off in  terms of your performance and this is  even truer if you're working out  multiple times per day every time I do  press ups I'm increasing protein  synthesis blood flow neural Drive in  those muscles and as such they're  getting a little spike and in these  subsequent hours they're more likely to  grow and build and get stronger of  course you could risk overtraining this  way but the whole point is that these  shorter workouts aren't really long  enough to create that risk in the first  place  at the same time I'm not saying you need  to train the same body part or even the  same skills in the same attributes you  can go for a run for your first  10-minute workout you could do bicep  curls later on in the day and then you  can do a bunch of pull-ups later on in  the evening and there's no reason that  should make you any more likely to  become fatigued than doing those things  all together in one hour  in fact you're less likely to become  fatigued less likely to injure yourself  because in between them you've had a  huge amount of time to rest and recover  it's then up to you if you want to do  full-body or if you want to do each body  part  I've talked on this channel in the past  about how I like to switch different  modalities use different tools use  different Cadence's so I might do  overcoming isometrics I might do  plyometrics I might do yielding  isometrics or I might just do regular  dynamic repetitions I can split these  into separate workouts and then mix them  up throughout the week and that way I'm  stimulating all kinds of different  things in all kinds of different ways  and that kind of flexibility allows it  to really focus on those things without  getting fatigued and without seeing my  performance drop off when I'm doing each  different aspect this kind of training  obviously lends itself really well to  the greasing the groove that I've been  talking about recently greasing the  groove means that you're going to do a  particular exercise regularly  normally one that's technically  demanding and thereby even though you  might not do enough repetitions in order  to trigger hypertrophy or in order to  cause muscle damage or anything like  that what you're doing is you're  lighting up the neural networks the  areas of the brain responsible for those  movements thereby you're creating  long-term potentiation strengthening the  neural connections and what this does it  allows you to move through that movement  more efficiently thereby exerting more  strength and also thereby learning new  skills that you might otherwise have  struggled with a perfect example is  handstands which I've been practicing a  lot lately I've just been doing three  sets of 20-second handstands whenever I  get a moment  this isn't enough to cause fatigue it  isn't causing any overtraining because  it's not enough of a stimulus to cause  that but it is enough of a stimulus to  light up those brain areas to strengthen  those connections and to ensure that I'm  getting slightly better at balancing  slightly better at listen to me the  prior section every single time I do it  and during my own experiments I can  definitely vouch for this because I've  become much better at doing those  handstands much more quickly than using  any other training method that I've used  in the past and yeah if you do the same  thing doing squats with a lighter weight  and you can become more efficient at  moving through the squat which means  that we can use a heavy weight later on  you'll be able to do it far better  what about hypertrophy what about muscle  endurance what about strength gains you  might be worried that 10 minutes or 20  minutes isn't enough to cause enough of  a stimulus to trigger adaptation and  growth but I disagree because pretty  much any kind of training that you need  to do any kind of stimulus you need can  be gained in just 10 to 20 minutes for  instance if you want to gain your  maximum strength on the bench press then  there's no reason that you can't lift  your 80 to 90 percent rep max for 3 to 4  reps then stop you might want to do a  few warm-up sets first maybe some foam  rolling or whatever but that whole thing  with 2 minute rest times in between  which should be about right that whole  thing is only going to take you 10 to 15  minutes really then you can do your  squats and your deadlift later on in the  day and you'll actually have far more  energy and drive to do those things  meaning that you probably want to start  with higher weight and see faster  progress some people might say aren't  you gonna get less of a testosterone  boost and less of an anabolic response  by doing shorter workouts like this and  that's a good question but actually it  doesn't really matter because when you  spike testosterone in your blood and  growth hormone in response to compound  exercises that doesn't lead to greater  strength gains this has been proven in  research  there'll be an article linked in the  description down below probably and I'll  link to all the studies that support  this in that but yeah the research shows  that these spikes in testosterone don't  lead to greater hypertrophy don't lead  to greater recovery or greater strength  gains you get a spike in testosterone  every time you have sex or watch an  action movie or win a game of cards and  you're not gonna get really massively  strong by doing that regularly so it  really doesn't matter whether you're  doing your 3 big lifts all in one  workout or at 3 different points  throughout the day likewise I've been  doing a lot of high volume calisthenics  lately I'll do a video on this soon so  in a quick workout I might do 150 air  squats I might do 150 press ups and  do 70 pull-ups this kind of thing only  takes about 15 minutes the point is that  I'm trying to keep the rest times as low  as possible to improve my recovery  that's what this is all about I'm trying  to getting pumped for size etc and  strengthen the tendons this short  workout once again definitely triggers  hypertrophy and definitely improves  muscle endurance and it's great for  mitochondria etc and it only takes that  short amount of time likewise if you're  interested in endurance performance and  mitochondria in particular then  obviously high-intensity interval  training may be a 2 bar to session with  a heavy kettlebell that's perfect for  triggering a definite response in a very  short amount of time tabata takes 4  minutes and this is also great for  weight loss and the best thing is again  here you're increasing your metabolism  and the afterburn effect so that extends  throughout the day because every time  you're going back to your baseline  you're getting more activity and again  your body is benefiting from not being  inert which can cause all kinds of  health problems if it's hypertrophy  you're interested in body building then  try running the rack with dumbbell curls  take a dumbbell do 12 reps to failure  then drop down the weight do 10 to  failure 8 to failure and a rest time in  between do that three times  that's enough stimulus to grow your  biceps there is no reason you can't be a  bodybuilder and split your routine up  like this do you think I can see being  difficult is steady-state cardio in  other words improving your ability to go  for a run for an hour this is the said  principle specific adaptation to impose  demands if you want to be able to exert  yourself for long periods then you kind  of need to exert yourself for a long  period using interval training with  longer rest times can trigger some of  the same benefits as steady-state cardio  because of inertia basically your heart  rate ramps up to 90 percent which is  more of a twitch they're not enough to  cause hypertrophy but then it also slows  down gradually meaning that if you go  for a walk maybe for two minutes that's  going to mean you're spending some time  with your heart rate at 70 and 60  percent going for long runs improves  other things as well though for instance  it helps to improve your ability to take  in oxygen you can mimic this a little  bit using something like a training mask  which I've talked about on this channel  before but yeah it's not gonna be as  good as going for long runs but if it is  something you'd like to incorporate them  perhaps that could be the exception to  the rule you go for a couple of long  runs and the rest of time you're  splitting your workouts throughout the  day so to conclude there's no good  reason why splitting your workouts  throughout the day would lead to lesser  gains in terms of aesthetics or in terms  of performance and in many cases it  would actually be beneficial  another exam  of something that actually would hugely  benefit from splitting the workouts up  is mobility same goes for things like  balance and for hand-eye coordination  all these things you can train an awful  lot I find this is a particularly good  way to integrate lots of different types  of training lots of different training  modes which I've talked about a lot on  this channel as well as to fit a lot of  training into a really busy and hectic  schedule but you won't want to pop to  the bathroom and splash yourself down  afterwards obvious is much easier for me  because I work from home so I recommend  that you all work from home obviously  failing that there are ways around this  such as to do your workouts in the  morning before work during lunch and in  the evening after work twice maybe you  have a caveat is that it's really  important that you make your training  sessions intense enough in order to get  the response that you're looking for the  shorter the amount of time you're doing  this workout in the more intense it  needs to be you need to do roughly the  same amount that you would do for that  particular trait or that particular  muscle group in order to trigger the  response looking to get the meed the  minimal effective dose this is something  you'll be able to sense with time as you  become more used to training and so  that's one arguments may be trying this  out once you're more accustomed to  training using a more conventional  method so yeah I'm gonna carry on  splitting my workouts into lots of  little bits I me know in the comments  down below if this is something you guys  might do I'd love to hear your thoughts  if you like this kind of alternative  philosophical cross modal approach to  training then you'll love my ebook and  training program super functional  training and you can find a link to that  in description down below you also go to  find a link to my website over there so  check it out and if you enjoyed this  video then please leave a like subscribe  share it around there helps me out  immensely thanks a ton for watching and  I'll see you next time bye for now
 

